  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p>The best approaches to achieve rolling deployments with minimal service impact while being cost-effective are <b>Re-deploy using CloudFormation and define update policies for Auto Scaling groups in your template</b> and <b>Utilize the AutoscalingRollingUpdate property to control how CloudFormation manages changes to your Auto Scaling Group</b>. Both solutions offer automated rolling updates and minimal downtime by leveraging CloudFormation's ability to gradually update or replace instances according to specified policies. The <b>AutoscalingRollingUpdate</b> attribute, as well as update policies on Auto Scaling groups, control how instances are replaced, preventing service outages and reducing cost by not doubling resource usage.</p><p>The option <i>Re-deploy your app through an OpsWorks template to launch Elastic Beanstalk</i> is incorrect because OpsWorks and Elastic Beanstalk are different deployment services and combining them in this way is inefficient and not standard practice. The option <i>After finishing a stack deployment, dismantle the previous stack immediately</i> risks downtime and does not support rolling, controlled deployment; tearing down the stack could abruptly terminate active resources, impacting end users.</p><p><b>Tip:</b> Use CloudFormation's update and rolling deployment policies for precise, controlled updates to Auto Scaling groups, and always review the options for controlling replacement behavior to reduce service interruptions.</p>",
  "query": "<p>You are acting as a DevOps specialist for a company. The team wants a cost-saving method to perform rolling deployments and minimize service interruptions, all while maintaining stack policies. What approaches should you implement? Select two responses from the options below.</p>",
